WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.720
A nanoscale platform now tracks single proteins moving inside living neurons in real time. This is Planetarian Daily, episode 219. RNA polymerase selection comes next, then isomer mapping on surfaces, with a science deep dive on how copying accuracy actually evolves. The University of Queensland built NASDAQ to watch disease-related proteins one molecule at a time inside living cells, according to Fizz.org. The platform logs where each protein sits

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.840
and when each molecular event happens.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:37.720
The work has been spun out into accretion bio, a Brisbane startup for early Alzheimer's

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.240
and Parkinson's drug discovery.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.940
It runs in intact neurons, not in cells that have been fixed.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:47.660
That gives a direct look at how given proteins act in healthy states and in disease.

00:00:48.841 --> 00:00:53.521
Older approaches either froze the cells in place or average signals across many molecules,

00:00:54.041 --> 00:00:55.701
so the order of events disappeared.

00:00:55.701 --> 00:01:01.201
The live single molecule record can show how specific proteins cluster or disperse during

00:01:01.201 --> 00:01:04.881
ordinary function and during early signs of neurodegeneration.

00:01:05.381 --> 00:01:09.901
Drug developers can test whether candidate compounds alter those patterns before visible

00:01:09.901 --> 00:01:11.001
cell damage occurs.

00:01:11.721 --> 00:01:14.321
The same platform works across different cell types.

00:01:14.781 --> 00:01:19.581
That comparison can ask whether the same proteins behave differently in neurons versus glia.

00:01:19.581 --> 00:01:27.619
An RNA polymerase ribozyme now copies functional RNAs with 94% fidelity per nucleotide, according

00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:29.639
to a BioRXIV preprint.

00:01:30.119 --> 00:01:32.679
33 rounds of selection produced that enzyme.

00:01:33.379 --> 00:01:38.339
The gain came from fewer mismatched incorporations and from fewer extensions of partial products

00:01:38.339 --> 00:01:39.759
that ended in mismatches.

00:01:40.279 --> 00:01:45.599
The enzyme also copies a wider range of templates, including highly structured sequences that earlier

00:01:45.599 --> 00:01:46.879
versions handled poorly.

00:01:46.879 --> 00:01:52.899
When tested on the Class I ligase ribosim, the new polymerase produced full-length functional

00:01:52.899 --> 00:01:54.159
copies more reliably.

00:01:54.759 --> 00:01:59.699
The work targets two long-standing limits on using RNA polymerases to replicate and evolve

00:01:59.699 --> 00:02:01.279
other functional RNAs.

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:04.539
Copying accuracy and template sequence generality.

00:02:05.454 --> 00:02:10.074
Something most people get wrong is the belief that an RNA copying enzyme is stuck with

00:02:10.074 --> 00:02:14.714
whatever accuracy its first fold allows, as if that number were sealed unless someone

00:02:14.714 --> 00:02:20.534
redesign the molecule from the outside. Laboratory selection can reshape how an RNA polymerase

00:02:20.534 --> 00:02:25.834
ribosome treats both the right nucleotide and the wrong one as a chain grows. The process

00:02:25.834 --> 00:02:31.174
starts with a mixed population of ribosomes that differ slightly in sequence. Each round puts

00:02:31.174 --> 00:02:35.874
that population under conditions that reward accurate copying of functional RNA templates.

00:02:36.574 --> 00:02:41.554
Variants that copy less accurately are discarded. Over successive rounds, the population

00:02:41.554 --> 00:02:45.414
converges on sequences that insert fewer mismatched nucleotides.

00:02:45.834 --> 00:02:49.854
Those sequences also stop extending when a mismatch sits at the growing end.

00:02:50.314 --> 00:02:53.514
Instead of pushing past the error, the enzyme that emerges

00:02:53.514 --> 00:02:56.874
keeps higher overall fidelity across long RNA products.

00:02:57.554 --> 00:03:03.234
Inside living systems, the same principle operates whenever natural selection acts on replication machinery.

00:03:03.854 --> 00:03:07.554
Variants that reduce error rates gain an advantage when accurate transmission

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:09.874
of genetic information improves survival.

00:03:09.874 --> 00:03:14.114
The laboratory version shows that fidelity is not an inherent ceiling.

00:03:14.714 --> 00:03:17.974
It is an outcome of the selective pressures applied during evolution.

00:03:18.614 --> 00:03:22.774
Further changes in selection conditions, such as different template lengths or added

00:03:22.774 --> 00:03:25.814
chemical modifications, could drive additional gains.

00:03:26.614 --> 00:03:29.214
Accuracy remains adjustable rather than locked.

00:03:29.774 --> 00:03:34.294
Future polymerase designs can be tuned systematically rather than discovered by chance.

00:03:34.294 --> 00:03:41.154
scientists at NPL, the University of Surrey, the University of Oxford, the Francis Crick

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:46.954
Institute, and IONTOF GMBH created a technique that maps molecules sharing the same chemical

00:03:46.954 --> 00:03:50.074
formula, yet differing in three-dimensional arrangement.

00:03:50.694 --> 00:03:54.694
The method distinguishes islamers that have the same mass, but different structures

00:03:54.694 --> 00:03:57.994
and functions, as reported today in nature communications.

00:03:58.634 --> 00:03:59.774
It works on surfaces.

00:04:00.414 --> 00:04:03.594
Spatial information is preserved while those molecules are mapped.

00:04:03.594 --> 00:04:08.354
Molecules that look identical by mass alone can now be told apart in place.

00:04:09.366 --> 00:04:14.106
University of Javascula chemists developed a strategy that direct synthetic molecules

00:04:14.106 --> 00:04:17.666
to self-assemble into defined three-dimensional triple helices.

00:04:18.286 --> 00:04:23.786
Fizz.org reports that the method gives control over the final architecture that earlier approaches lacked.

00:04:24.406 --> 00:04:29.366
Artificial systems move closer to the precise organization seen in natural macro molecules.

00:04:29.366 --> 00:04:33.666
The study brings that level of order into molecules built from scratch.

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:40.230
Honey Bee Hives maintain multiple antimicrobial defenses that protect the colony, its brood

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.570
and stored food according to Fizz.org.

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:46.130
The layered system reduces disease spread inside the hive.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:52.250
Those defenses act as a living immune system for the colony, its offspring and its food supplies.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:56.350
Protecting Bee Habitat is tied to keeping that layered defense intact.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:05:01.751
In fact, birds transport nutrients from land, lakes and sea through waste and carcasses

00:05:02.291 --> 00:05:06.431
according to a Fizz.org report on a review of nearly 400 studies.

00:05:06.931 --> 00:05:10.971
Researchers from Cornell and five partner institutions compiled the synthesis.

00:05:11.651 --> 00:05:16.511
The movement alters soil chemistry, water chemistry, plant growth, and animal communities

00:05:16.511 --> 00:05:17.871
far from nesting sites.

00:05:18.391 --> 00:05:21.631
The effect spans coral reefs, forests, and open ocean.

00:05:21.631 --> 00:05:26.471
Bird's distribute nutrients at large scale and sustain life well beyond the nest.

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:32.430
John Innis Center researchers trained scientists from across Tanzania to use loop-mediated

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:37.230
isothermal amplification, or lamp assays according to Fizz.org.

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:40.250
The tool rapidly detects wheat-blast fungus.

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:44.290
Wheat-blast is a looming fungal threat to wheat harvests in the region.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.070
Training covered both field and laboratory use of the assays.

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:53.824
Full implementation of current national methane plans could reduce global emissions by 11

00:05:53.824 --> 00:06:00.844
percent between 2020 and 2030, according to an IIA-ASA-led analysis reported by FIS.org.

00:06:01.424 --> 00:06:03.664
UNEP colleagues co-authored the study.

00:06:04.204 --> 00:06:06.924
The work combined government pledges with emissions modeling.

00:06:07.484 --> 00:06:09.984
That path would reverse the recent rise in emissions.

00:06:10.664 --> 00:06:13.924
The reduction still falls short of the global methane pledge target.

00:06:13.924 --> 00:06:19.274
William and Mary's Virginia Institute of Marine Science recorded average numbers of young

00:06:19.274 --> 00:06:24.594
of year striped bass in Chesapeake Bay tributaries this spring, according to Fizz.org.

00:06:25.134 --> 00:06:29.494
Preliminary results point to an average year class produced in the Virginia tributaries

00:06:29.494 --> 00:06:30.514
in 2026.

00:06:31.134 --> 00:06:35.174
Fish hatched in 2026 will reach fishable size in three to four years.

00:06:35.794 --> 00:06:38.894
Striped bass serve as top predators in the Bay ecosystem.

00:06:39.474 --> 00:06:42.314
They support both commercial and recreational fisheries.

00:06:42.314 --> 00:06:46.814
The ongoing long-term survey supplies the data used for management decisions.

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:53.130
A temporary expansion of the earned income tax credit, also today, reduced housing insecurity

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:56.770
among young workers without children, according to Fizz.org.

00:06:57.370 --> 00:07:03.150
New mapping identified 13% of Earth's land as biodiversity conservation hotspots ahead

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.770
of the UN biodiversity conference in Armenia.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:11.930
Before we go, the next concrete step for the improved RNA polymerase ribosim is testing

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:16.870
Whether it holds fidelity on longer functional RNAs and inside model cells.

